 "script": "In Germany, breaking out of prison is not a crime. Giving the escapee a ride is. [disclaimer] Meet Klaus. Klaus is serving a sentence in a German prison. One night, Klaus finds a door left unlocked. And he walks out. His friend Jonas is waiting outside with a car. Klaus gets in. A few days later, police catch them both. And both end up in court. German criminal law has no crime called escaping. But section one twenty punishes anyone who frees a prisoner, talks one into leaving, or helps. So Jonas, the driver, faces up to three years. For an official whose job is to stop escapes, it's up to five. And Klaus? Nothing new. Not for the escape itself. Wait. The man who escaped is fine, and the man who drove is the criminal? You see, German law treats the urge to be free as human nature. It's like a bird flying out of an open cage. Nobody blames the bird. But if you open the cage, that's on you. The sentence still stands. Klaus gets brought back, faces prison discipline, and early release gets harder. Damage the bars or hurt a guard, and that's its own crime. So Klaus goes back to his cell with no new charge. And the only new criminal in the story is the guy who gave him a ride. [loop] So if you ever hear someone say breaking out is legal, remember",
